# Line endings, declared rather than left to each machine's git config.
#
# Without this file, what lands in a checkout depends on the cloner's
# core.autocrlf. This repository was built on Windows with autocrlf on, so the
# working tree is CRLF while a Linux clone would be LF — and two committed
# files had ended up with *both* inside them, because pandas writes CRLF on
# Windows while the run-log appender writes LF deliberately (it writes bytes to
# an O_APPEND descriptor, so it cannot use platform translation).
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` means: git decides what is text, normalises it to LF in
# the repository, and checks it out as LF everywhere. Python, markdown and CSV
# are all fine with LF on Windows. The one exception is below.
* text=auto eol=lf

# PowerShell must stay CRLF. Windows PowerShell 5.1 reads a .ps1 as ANSI unless
# it has a UTF-8 BOM, and is unreliable with bare LF; the clean-clone script is
# BOM-prefixed and ASCII-only for the same reason.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Explicitly text, so a diff is readable and normalisation applies.
*.py    text
*.md    text
*.toml  text
*.txt   text
*.csv   text
*.jsonl text
*.ipynb text
*.cfg   text
.gitignore      text
.gitattributes  text
.env.example    text

# Binary, so git never tries to normalise or diff them as text.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.pdf binary
*.xlsx binary
